const confuciusSections: ArticleSection[] = [
  section('evidence','A historical teacher behind many remembered figures',[
    'Confucius, or Kongzi, is traditionally dated 551–479 BCE and associated with the state of Lu during the late Spring and Autumn period. Those dates provide a conventional orientation, not a modern documentary biography. Early sources support the existence of a teacher known for ritual expertise, learning, political counsel, and a circle of students, but later accounts organize his life into an exemplary career. Sima Qian’s much later biography supplies influential stories about ancestry, offices, travels, disciples, disappointment, and death. Such stories belong to the history of Confucius, yet their moral and literary shaping must remain visible.',
    'The distinction is not between a real Confucius and worthless legend. Didactic anecdotes show how early communities remembered what a teacher should be, while a few external chronological notices offer firmer anchors. The problem is circularity: interpreters have often used stories to explain sayings and sayings to certify stories. This profile therefore calls the lifespan traditional, treats senior office and extensive interstate travel cautiously, and avoids a precise developmental chronology. Confucius’s historical importance does not depend on recovering a diary. It rests on the unusually fertile traditions of practice, dialogue, criticism, and interpretation that gathered around Master Kong.',
  ],['confucianism','chinese-philosophy'],[],['Analects']),
  section('analects','The Analects as a formed and transmitted collection',[
    'The Analects is a collection of short sayings, exchanges, descriptions, and remembered scenes arranged in twenty received books. It does not identify one author or narrate a continuous life. Traditional accounts credit disciples and later followers with collecting the Master’s words, while modern scholarship detects different vocabularies, interests, and compositional layers. Confucius-related sayings circulated more broadly before the received collection stabilized, and excavated manuscripts have complicated any simple story of direct dictation. The text is therefore the central witness to the Analects tradition without being a stenographic record in which every sentence can automatically be assigned to the historical Confucius.',
    'Transmission also shaped what later readers encountered. Early imperial sources knew variant Lu, Qi, and Old Text recensions; the received text emerged through collation and commentary. Passage numbering is a modern aid imposed on the book divisions, and English translations make consequential choices for terms such as ren, li, junzi, de, and tian. A careful reader cites book and passage, names the translation when wording matters, and compares renderings rather than building doctrine on one memorable English phrase. Internal tensions need not be errors: they may record different speakers, contexts, stages of compilation, or a pedagogy resistant to universal formulas.',
  ],['confucianism','chinese-philosophy'],[],['Analects']),
  section('learning','Learning as repeated, social practice',[
    'The Analects begins by linking learning with timely practice, companionship, and composure when one is not recognized. Learning is not merely acquiring propositions. It includes studying inherited forms, observing conduct, asking how words fit roles, rehearsing ritual, responding to music and poetry, and correcting oneself among companions. Confucius repeatedly presents himself as devoted to learning rather than as someone born with complete knowledge. This posture joins intellectual humility to persistence: one can take pleasure in refinement while remaining alert to the vanity of displaying erudition or seeking office simply for status.',
    'The exemplary person, often translating junzi, is correspondingly an achieved ethical type rather than simply a hereditary noble. The inherited aristocratic word is redirected toward conduct: steadiness, responsiveness, trustworthiness, courage governed by judgment, and willingness to learn from others. Yet the Analects does not offer a checklist that makes character mechanical. Particular virtues can become distorted in isolation; courage without right orientation becomes disorder, and candor without ritual form becomes harshness. The learner must perceive what a relationship and occasion require. Moral knowledge is therefore practical, relational, and revisable, even while inherited models give it direction.',
  ],['ethics','confucianism'],[],['Analects']),
  section('ren-li','Humaneness and ritual are mutually forming',[
    'Ren is often translated as humaneness, benevolence, or authoritative humanity. No single English equivalent captures all its uses. Sometimes it names a demanding comprehensive excellence; elsewhere it appears through care, restraint, loyalty, reciprocity, or the refusal to impose on others what one would not accept oneself. The Analects answers questions about ren differently according to the questioner. That variation suggests pedagogical diagnosis rather than a hidden dictionary definition. Humaneness becomes visible in how a person attends to others, bears responsibilities, and reshapes desire, not in professing a warm feeling or applying an abstract slogan.',
    'Li, ritual or ritual propriety, includes ceremonies but extends to patterned conduct, mourning, greeting, deference, music, gift exchange, and the embodied grammar of roles. Confucius does not treat correct motion as sufficient: ritual without reverence or grief can be hollow. Nor does inward sincerity make form dispensable. Practices train attention and emotion, coordinate expectations, and give care public expression. The productive tension between ren and li guards against two reductions. Confucian cultivation is neither blind conformity to inherited etiquette nor private benevolence unconstrained by shared forms. It asks how forms can educate feeling and how humane judgment can keep forms ethically alive.',
  ],['ethics','confucianism'],[],['Analects']),
  section('family','Family roles, reciprocity, and their limits',[
    'Family relationships are a primary training ground in the Analects. Filial conduct involves material support, but passages insist that animals too can be fed; respect, demeanor, remembrance, and responsible remonstrance matter. The family is not simply a private refuge from politics. It teaches modes of attention that can extend into public responsibility, and ancestral rites locate persons within obligations across generations. At the same time, modern readers should not convert this account into universal approval of every historical hierarchy. The received text emerged in a patriarchal social world and gives unequal attention to different voices and roles.',
    'A famous exchange about uprightness contrasts reporting a father’s theft with mutual concealment between father and son. It raises a genuine conflict among impartial law, loyalty, reform, and relational trust; it should not be flattened into permission for corruption. Elsewhere a child is expected to remonstrate gently with parents rather than obey without judgment. Confucian role ethics therefore contains resources for responsibility inside attachment, even though interpreters disagree about how far those resources challenge abusive power. Contemporary appropriation must distinguish the insight that agency is relational from the stronger claim that inherited family authority is always legitimate.',
  ],['ethics','political-philosophy'],[],['Analects']),
  section('government','Government by exemplary power and corrected roles',[
    'Political order in the Analects begins with the conduct of rulers and officials. Government by coercive punishments may produce avoidance without shame, whereas guidance through virtue and ritual can cultivate a sense of appropriate conduct. The contrast is one of emphasis, not a complete institutional constitution or an assertion that laws never matter. Confucius addresses courts in which hereditary rank, fragile alliances, warfare, and usurpation had damaged received norms. His appeal to exemplary power asks officeholders to become credible centers of trust rather than demand compliance while exempting themselves from discipline.',
    'The “rectification of names” passage connects language with action: if names do not fit realities and roles, speech, affairs, ritual, justice, and punishment lose coordination. This is not merely a campaign for fixed definitions. A ruler must act as a ruler should; titles carry normative expectations that performance can betray. Confucius also values employing capable people, keeping promises, and securing basic trust. Yet the text is not democratic in a modern sense, and exemplary rule can become paternalism when detached from remonstrance and material conditions. Its enduring question is how public authority earns normative force through accountable practice.',
  ],['political-philosophy','philosophy-of-language'],[],['Analects']),
  section('tian','Heaven, fate, spirits, and practical reverence',[
    'Tian, conventionally rendered Heaven, appears as moral order, inherited authority, circumstance, and a power connected with Confucius’s vocation. The Analects does not supply one systematic theology. Confucius can speak with confidence that Heaven understands him while also recognizing ming, what is allotted or beyond control. These passages should not be translated automatically into a personal creator God, impersonal nature, or secular fate. Their philosophical force lies partly in joining committed action with limits: one works on character and office without imagining that recognition, success, or political outcome is wholly controllable.',
    'The text recommends respect toward spirits while keeping distance and records reluctance to discuss prodigies. It also insists that one cannot serve spirits properly without learning to serve human beings. This is neither straightforward atheism nor proof of uncritical supernaturalism. Ritual address to ancestors and spirits belongs to the practice-world of the text, while its pedagogical emphasis remains on conduct among the living. Modern readers should resist making Confucius either a secular humanist ahead of his culture or a dogmatic theologian. His reserve directs attention toward reverent practice, moral agency, and epistemic restraint.',
  ],['philosophy-of-religion','confucianism'],[],['Analects']),
  section('music-poetry','Music, poetry, emotion, and cultivated judgment',[
    'Poetry and music are not decorative additions to moral philosophy. The Songs offer language for feeling and social observation, while music orders tempo, attention, and shared affect. Confucius is represented as judging performances by both beauty and ethical character, and as treating poetry as a resource for stimulation, observation, sociability, and complaint. Such practices educate perception: the learner acquires ways to notice excess, restraint, grief, joy, and the consequences of conduct. This helps explain why ritual cultivation cannot be reduced to rule following; ethical responsiveness requires an attuned emotional and imaginative repertoire.',
    'Claims that Confucius personally edited every classic or fixed a canonical curriculum belong largely to later tradition and should be qualified. The Analects certainly associates him with Songs, Documents, rites, and music, but association does not prove sole authorship or final redaction. His transmitted image is better understood as teacher and interpreter of inherited cultural materials. That role is philosophically significant: learning can conserve the past without merely repeating it, because performance and judgment renew meaning in changed circumstances. The difficult question is how to distinguish creative fidelity from nostalgic authorization of an unequal order.',
  ],['aesthetics','ethics'],[],['Analects','Classic of Poetry']),
  section('afterlives','A source repeatedly remade by later traditions',[
    'Confucius did not found one continuous institution already identical with everything later called Confucianism. Mencius and Xunzi developed competing accounts of cultivation and human nature; imperial states connected classics and offices in changing ways; Han commentators, Song thinkers, examination cultures, family ritualists, critics, and modern reformers selected different Confucian figures. Zhu Xi’s inclusion of the Analects among the Four Books profoundly shaped reading without simply recovering an original school. The label “Confucian” is useful for reception when it does not erase plurality or project later systems backward into the fifth century BCE.',
    'Modern global images have been equally selective. Confucius has served as emblem of hierarchy, education, civic virtue, authoritarian order, family responsibility, cultural identity, and dialogical ethics. Missionaries and Enlightenment writers recast him through European categories; twentieth-century movements attacked and rehabilitated him; contemporary philosophers compare role-based cultivation with virtue ethics and care ethics. Comparison can illuminate, but calling ren an exact Chinese version of one Western virtue or the Analects a handbook for corporate harmony removes historical friction. Reception is best studied as transformation: later communities inherit questions and textual resources, not a timeless package.',
  ],['confucianism','chinese-philosophy'],['mencius','xunzi','zhu-xi','wang-yangming'],['Analects']),
  section('reading','A reading path that preserves textual difficulty',[
    'Begin with one complete modern annotated translation of the Analects, reading slowly by book and passage rather than extracting quotations. Mark who asks each question, compare repeated terms, and notice when answers vary. Use the Chinese Text Project to locate the received Chinese and standard divisions, but do not treat its older English rendering as the only possible translation. Then consult a specialist account of sources and transmission. This order lets the text remain a collection of situated exchanges while giving readers enough historical orientation to avoid treating every scene as direct biography.',
    'Next compare Slingerland and Eno on ren, li, junzi, de, tian, learning, and government. Read selected traditional commentaries to see how later interpreters made tensions productive. Hunter’s study then widens the dossier beyond the Analects and challenges the assumption that one received anthology exhausts early Confucius discourse. Keep three notebooks: textual observation, historical confidence, and present philosophical response. This separation permits serious contemporary engagement without disguising a modern preference as ancient fact. The goal is not to find an instant slogan but to learn how practice, relationship, language, and judgment interact.',
    'As a final exercise, follow one theme across passages that do not immediately agree. On governing, compare moral example, sufficient food and arms, trust, naming, education, and punishment; on filial conduct, compare support, respectful demeanor, remonstrance, mourning, and concealment. Ask whether a difference reflects audience, context, compilation, or genuine tension before harmonizing it. Then compare how Mencius and Xunzi appropriate the inheritance. Neither later thinker supplies a master key, but their disagreements reveal possibilities already opened by a dialogical collection. Reading this way turns inconsistency from an embarrassment into an invitation to disciplined interpretation while preserving the difference between early evidence and later system building.',
    'Translation comparison is part of that discipline. Choose two disputed passages and list where translators preserve a Chinese term, select an English virtue word, or import grammatical subjects absent from the compact original. Consult commentary before announcing which version is correct. This practice reveals interpretation at work without requiring beginners to pretend to philological expertise. It also protects the Analects from a quotation culture that circulates polished English maxims without book number, translator, textual context, or transmission history.',
  ],['confucianism','chinese-philosophy','ethics'],[],['Analects']),
];

