const menciusSections: ArticleSection[] = [
  section('life-text','A Warring States persuader known through a named book',[
    'Mencius is the Latinized name of Mengzi, Master Meng, conventionally dated about 372–289 BCE. The received work depicts him traveling among Warring States courts, debating rulers and rivals, teaching disciples, and defending a Confucian way associated with Confucius. Exact dates and an orderly itinerary remain uncertain. Later biography adds family stories, especially the exemplary mother who moved homes to improve his environment. These stories became culturally powerful illustrations of cultivation, but they should not be treated as contemporary documentation. The historically responsible core is a prominent fourth-century BCE teacher whose remembered conversations address rule, livelihood, virtue, desire, and argument.',
    'The Mengzi consists of seven books, each divided into two parts, transmitted as dialogues, speeches, anecdotes, and arguments. Tradition associates disciples with compilation, but the collection’s formation cannot be reconstructed as a verbatim record. Its literary Mencius is a forceful persuader who adapts examples to audiences and sometimes stages opponents sharply. Standard passage numbers allow comparison across translations; English wording should still be tied to a named translator. The text’s coherence is substantial enough for philosophical interpretation, yet readers should not assume that every passage belongs to one moment, one rhetorical genre, or a system written from beginning to end.',
  ],['confucianism','chinese-philosophy'],['confucius'],['Mencius']),
  section('nature','What it means to say human nature is good',[
    'The slogan that human nature is good can mislead if it is heard as “everyone is already morally excellent” or “people automatically choose well.” Mencius distinguishes the beginnings or sprouts of virtue from their mature achievement. Humans characteristically have affective responses that can grow into humaneness, rightness, ritual propriety, and wisdom when supported and extended. Bad environments, deprivation, neglect, and repeated harmful action can damage or bury these tendencies. Goodness is therefore developmental and normative: human capacities point toward forms of flourishing, but their realization is neither guaranteed nor independent of social conditions.',
    'Nature, xing, is interpreted through examples of growth, appetite, shared capacities, and characteristic development. Scholars dispute whether Mencius claims an innate moral essence, a natural tendency under suitable conditions, or a normative account of distinctively human completion. The sprout image favors neither blank-slate instruction nor finished virtue. A sprout is real, fragile, directionally organized, and dependent on nourishment. This is why Mencius can condemn wrongdoing without imagining a demonic substance inside the wrongdoer. Failure may involve lost attention, starved capacities, destructive habituation, coercive conditions, or refusing to extend a response one already recognizes.',
  ],['ethics','philosophy-of-mind'],['xunzi'],['Mencius']),
  section('sprouts','Four beginnings as affective judgments',[
    'The best-known illustration asks readers to imagine suddenly seeing a child about to fall into a well. The immediate alarm and concern is not explained by seeking favor, praise, or avoidance of blame. Mencius uses the case to identify a beginning of humaneness, not to prove that every impulse is altruistic or that rescue will always occur. Parallel beginnings are a sense of shame or aversion, deference, and approval or disapproval. Each is an incipient way of registering a situation, joining emotion, perception, and evaluation before it becomes a stable virtue.',
    'These beginnings require extension. Concern vividly elicited by one child must become appropriately responsive across less immediate cases; shame must be educated so that convention does not attach it to the wrong objects; deference must not become servility; discriminating approval must mature through reflection. Cultivation is thus not simply obeying feeling. It involves attending, comparing, practicing, and enlarging ethically promising responses. Nor do the four beginnings map without remainder onto a modern faculty psychology. They are pedagogical patterns in a classical Chinese vocabulary whose translations—sprouts, hearts, feelings, or dispositions—highlight different aspects.',
  ],['ethics','philosophy-of-mind'],[],['Mencius']),
  section('cultivation','Nourishment, reflection, and the danger of forced growth',[
    'Mencius compares moral growth with tending crops. One must not neglect the shoots, but neither can one help them by pulling them upward, as the man of Song disastrously does. The parable rejects frantic self-manipulation as well as passivity. Reliable practice accumulates right action until one’s orientation has greater stability. The text also describes preserving or recovering the heart, reducing desires in some contexts, reflecting on what matters, and taking delight in fitting action. These are overlapping exercises rather than a single program with measurable stages.',
    'Material and political conditions remain crucial. The Ox Mountain image describes once-lush growth repeatedly cut down, grazed, and then mistaken for natural barrenness. Likewise, recurrent damage can make a person appear never to have possessed humane tendencies. This environmental account prevents moral psychology from becoming blame of isolated individuals. Yet Mencius preserves agency: people can seek the lost heart and extend what they know. The balance is difficult. Circumstances shape attention and habit profoundly, but explanation does not erase responsibility; responsibility is graded by access to nourishment, education, security, and opportunities for reflective repair.',
  ],['ethics','political-philosophy'],[],['Mencius']),
  section('yi-desire','Rightness, benefit, desire, and integrity',[
    'Mencius famously opens a royal conversation by asking why the king speaks of profit rather than humaneness and rightness. He does not deny every benefit or practical calculation. His warning concerns a political vocabulary in which each rank pursues advantage against the others, dissolving trust and public purpose. Yi, often rendered rightness or appropriateness, identifies what fits morally rather than what merely serves immediate gain. A cultivated person can value life while refusing survival on degrading terms, as the fish-and-bear-paw comparison dramatizes. Integrity reorganizes desire; it does not require having no desires.',
    'This account challenges the picture of reason battling an alien emotional body. Mencian ethical responses are affective and embodied, while reflection helps identify, extend, and rank them. The “flood-like qi” passage is especially difficult: qi can evoke vital energy, embodied disposition, or psychophysical vitality, and no single English equivalent settles its role. It grows through accumulated right action and is injured by conduct that does not satisfy the heart. Readers should resist turning it into a measurable substance or supernatural power. The passage connects ethical consistency with an embodied confidence that cannot be acquired through one dramatic performance.',
  ],['ethics','philosophy-of-mind'],[],['Mencius']),
  section('argument','Analogies, dilemmas, and moral attention',[
    'Mencius rarely argues through detached premises alone. He tells stories, asks rulers to redescribe their conduct, draws analogies, distinguishes words, and exposes inconsistency between professed values and policy. When King Xuan spares an ox he sees but not unseen people, Mencius treats the response as evidence of a capacity that has not been extended. The point is not that one feeling automatically dictates fiscal or military policy. It is that moral failure can arise from patterned attention: proximity activates concern while institutions keep comparable suffering out of view.',
    'Some arguments remain contestable. Analogies between plants and persons may presuppose the developmental direction they are meant to explain, and appeals to common reactions admit variation. Mencius also sometimes portrays rivals in ways that serve polemic. Philosophical respect does not require declaring every analogy demonstrative. His distinctive strength lies in showing that argument can rearrange perception and motivation. A successful exchange makes an audience see its existing commitments differently, revealing resources for change without pretending that persuasion alone repairs institutions. This rhetorical dimension belongs to the philosophy rather than decorating an independently complete theory.',
  ],['logic','ethics','political-philosophy'],[],['Mencius']),
  section('politics','Humane government and the material basis of virtue',[
    'Mencius tells rulers that stable livelihood supports stable moral life for ordinary people. Crushing taxes, hunger, insecure land, and warfare predictably damage families and invite crime; punishing the resulting conduct is compared to trapping the people. Humane government therefore includes material policies, light burdens, timely agriculture, care for vulnerable people, and education. The argument does not reduce virtue to economics, because cultivation still matters. It denies that rulers may produce desperation and then interpret its effects as evidence that the people naturally lack moral capacity.',
    'Political legitimacy is conditional. Mencius invokes the people’s importance, Heaven’s mandate, and the removal of destructive rulers; in the famous exchange about the last Shang king, he says he has heard of punishing “the fellow Zhou,” not murdering a ruler. This demotes a tyrant normatively before discussing his death. It is not a modern doctrine of electoral sovereignty, yet neither is it unconditional obedience. Ministers should remonstrate, rulers must secure humane conditions, and extreme failure can dissolve the moral standing attached to a title. Later political uses require attention to who judges failure and what prevents opportunistic rebellion.',
  ],['political-philosophy','ethics'],[],['Mencius']),
  section('rivals','Gaozi, Mohists, Yang Zhu, and Xunzi',[
    'The dialogues with Gaozi explore competing pictures of nature, desire, rightness, and cultivation. Gaozi compares nature to willow wood shaped into cups or water directed east and west; Mencius objects that making cups violates the wood and that water naturally flows downward when unobstructed. These images do argumentative work but do not yield a modern biological theory. Elsewhere Mencius attacks positions associated with Mozi and Yang Zhu as threatening differentiated family care or public obligation. The polemics are historically important while remaining Mencius’s representations of rivals, not neutral summaries of their complete views.',
    'Xunzi later argues that people’s spontaneous dispositions lead toward conflict unless transformed through deliberate effort, teachers, standards, ritual, and institutions. The contrast with Mencius is real, but “good versus evil” is too crude. They may weight different senses of xing, different political dangers, and different parts of moral development. Mencius also requires learning and environment; Xunzi also thinks human beings possess capacities through which transformation is possible. Their dispute asks whether ethical norms grow by extending native beginnings, reshape initially unruly tendencies, or do both in different respects. Translation should preserve that live question rather than decide it with slogans.',
  ],['confucianism','mohism','ethics'],['xunzi','mozi'],['Mencius','Xunzi','Mozi']),
  section('reception','Canonization and interpretive plurality',[
    'The Mengzi did not immediately occupy the later canonical rank it would receive. Over centuries, commentators and political thinkers used it in disputes about rule, human nature, education, and classical authority. Zhu Xi made it one of the Four Books and interpreted its moral psychology within a larger Neo-Confucian framework, helping make Mencius central to examination culture across East Asia. That victory should not be projected backward as if Mencius were always the uncontested orthodox successor to Confucius. Xunzian, Han, Buddhist, and other intellectual lineages produced different priorities and criticisms.',
    'Modern readers have compared Mencius with virtue ethics, moral sentimentalism, developmental psychology, care ethics, and theories of political legitimacy. Such comparisons can clarify questions about affective perception and cultivated agency, but exact equivalence is risky. The heart-mind, xin, is not simply a Western emotion faculty opposed to reason; ren and yi do not map neatly onto benevolence and justice as fixed modern categories. Contemporary appeal also raises criticism of hierarchy, gender, family authority, and paternal rule. A productive reception treats Mencius as an argumentative partner whose resources and exclusions both require scrutiny.',
  ],['confucianism','chinese-philosophy'],['zhu-xi','wang-yangming'],['Mencius']),
  section('reading','Reading beyond the four-sprouts slogan',[
    'Begin with Mengzi 1A for encounters with rulers, then read 2A for cultivation and qi, 3A–3B for economic and political debate, and 6A for the concentrated arguments about nature. Use a modern annotated translation such as Van Norden’s and keep the standard book-part-passage divisions visible. Compare the received Chinese through the Chinese Text Project when a key term recurs, but do not infer meaning from a dictionary gloss alone. Read the child-at-the-well, Ox Mountain, farmer of Song, King Xuan, and Gaozi passages in their argumentative neighborhoods.',
    'Then use Shun’s specialist overview to map the relation among heart-mind, sprouts, virtue, and self-cultivation, and Van Norden’s overview to test competing interpretations. Behuniak offers a process-oriented account that helps resist treating goodness as a hidden moral object already complete at birth. Finally compare Xunzi chapter 23, asking what each thinker includes under nature and what role education plays. The best reading path keeps psychology and politics together: a ruler’s institutions nourish or damage capacities, while persons still practice attention, extension, reflection, and right action.',
    'Test every summary against a difficult case. If people have beginnings of goodness, explain cultivated cruelty, selective compassion, and the effects of hunger without declaring the beginnings fictitious. If extension is central, ask what licenses movement from a vivid response to a distant policy and what institutions help attention travel. If rulers lose standing through tyranny, ask who can identify that threshold without turning any defeat into proof of lost mandate. Mencius is most philosophically useful when these questions remain demanding. His images orient inquiry, but they do not relieve readers of examining counterexamples, social exclusions, translation choices, or the political risks of paternal moral authority.',
    'Also distinguish the text’s own historical examples from the conclusions later readers draw from them. Idealized sage kings, agrarian arrangements, family roles, and court audiences carry Warring States assumptions that cannot simply be reproduced as policy. Ask which premise does the enduring work: that livelihood affects agency, that office is conditional on humane performance, that attention can be educated, or that differentiated relationships matter. Those claims can enter contemporary debate only after their original institutional setting and exclusions are visible. Responsible application is reconstruction with reasons, not the replacement of “Mencius says” by a preferred modern program.',
  ],['confucianism','ethics','political-philosophy'],['xunzi'],['Mencius','Xunzi']),
];

