const xunziSections: ArticleSection[] = [
  section('life-text','A teacher and a layered argumentative collection',[
    'Xunzi, Master Xun, is conventionally identified with Xun Kuang and placed about 310–235 BCE, though the endpoints are approximate. Later sources associate him with study at the Jixia academy in Qi, service in Chu, and teaching figures later connected with the Han Feizi and imperial administration. These reports give a plausible Warring States setting but not a fully documented itinerary. His lifetime saw intense interstate competition, administrative expansion, warfare, and debate among Confucian, Mohist, Daoist, and other positions. That context helps explain his insistence that ethical order is a difficult human achievement.',
    'The received Xunzi contains thirty-two chapters in a relatively sustained essay style, unusually expansive for early Chinese philosophy. It is not simply a book written once in final order. Scholars distinguish chapters more securely connected with Xunzi from materials shaped by students or later transmitters, and the collection was edited over time. Chapter titles remain useful stable locators without certifying single authorship of every line. A responsible profile treats the collection as the central Xunzian textual corpus, marks prominent textual problems, and avoids deriving a precise biography or chronological development from the present chapter sequence.',
  ],['confucianism','chinese-philosophy'],['mencius'],['Xunzi']),
  section('learning','Learning changes what spontaneous life alone cannot',[
    '“Encouraging Learning” opens with the claim that learning must not stop and develops it through images of wood bent by steaming, metal sharpened by grinding, accumulated earth, water, and steps. These analogies emphasize transformation through sustained process, teachers, models, textual study, reflection, and practice. Learning is not the passive storage of sayings. It reorganizes perception, desire, posture, speech, and action until a person can respond reliably. The chapter also warns that scattered effort achieves little. Ethical formation requires cumulative attention and participation in a community whose standards exceed one’s current preferences.',
    'The analogies can sound like external violence imposed on inert material, but Xunzi’s account is more complex. Humans possess intelligence, capacities for distinction, desires, emotions, and an ability to participate in norms. Deliberate effort works through those capacities even though the ethical patterns are not fully given in spontaneous impulse. Teachers and ritual models are therefore essential without making learners mechanically obedient. Study reaches completion in conduct. Memorizing classics while acting badly is failure, and clever disputation can fragment the Way. Knowledge matters when it becomes embodied competence governed by appropriate standards.',
  ],['ethics','epistemology'],[],['Xunzi']),
  section('nature','“Human nature is bad” as an argument, not a verdict of depravity',[
    'Chapter 23 is commonly translated “Human Nature Is Bad” or “Human Nature Is Evil,” but the term e can mark what is bad, ugly, or ethically disordered rather than an inherited metaphysical stain. Xing names characteristics present without deliberate learning, including desires and affective responses. Xunzi argues that following these tendencies without measure tends toward contention, violence, and social disorder. He does not claim that every spontaneous act is maximally vicious, that infants are blameworthy, or that people lack all capacity for goodness. His target is the claim that mature ethical order simply unfolds from native moral beginnings.',
    'The opposing slogan “human nature is good” is associated with Mencius, yet the texts may partly classify capacities differently. Xunzi locates goodness in deliberate effort, wei, and in socially created patterns; Mencius includes incipient moral responses within nature. Both think cultivation and supportive conditions matter. Xunzi’s sharper point is explanatory: if ritual and rightness were already complete in nature, why would sages labor to establish teachers, distinctions, and institutions? Critics reply that the ability to recognize and adopt good standards may itself require ethically promising tendencies. The debate concerns the sources and organization of normativity, not whether education is needed.',
  ],['ethics','philosophy-of-mind'],['mencius'],['Xunzi','Mencius']),
  section('desire','Desires require ordering, not annihilation',[
    'Xunzi does not teach that ethical life eliminates desire. Desires arise even in a cultivated person; wisdom concerns how one responds and how institutions distribute their objects. The heart-mind can approve, reject, compare, and direct action, but it is vulnerable to fixation, partiality, and obsession. Ritual creates measured forms through which desires can be satisfied without endless competition. Mourning, consumption, rank, music, and exchange receive limits and expressions proportionate to circumstances. Order therefore redirects and coordinates affect rather than demanding an impossible absence of appetite.',
    'This is why “bad nature” should not be reduced to hatred of the body. Xunzi’s psychology is embodied and social. Sensory faculties encounter objects, the heart-mind forms discriminations, and accumulated practice stabilizes conduct. Problems arise when one desire or perspective monopolizes attention and obscures the larger pattern. His famous discussion of dispelling blindness treats rival doctrines and private fixations as cognitive as well as moral failures. An integrated view can acknowledge desires without becoming captive to one of them. Ethical freedom is achieved organization, not release from every constraint or obedience to whatever first appears.',
  ],['ethics','philosophy-of-mind','epistemology'],[],['Xunzi']),
  section('ritual','Ritual as a human answer to scarcity and feeling',[
    'Xunzi explains ritual through a genealogy: people are born with desires; shared pursuit of limited goods produces contention; contention produces disorder and poverty. Ancient sages established ritual and standards to create distinctions, allocations, and forms of satisfaction. This is not merely etiquette layered over an otherwise complete morality. Ritual makes coordinated social life possible and gives desires shapes in which they can be expressed without consuming the community. Rank and distribution remain hierarchical, so modern readers must evaluate both the coordinating insight and the inequalities built into the proposed order.',
    'Ritual also refines emotion. In mourning, formal stages, clothing, sound, and duration give grief an adequate beginning and end. Artifice does not make feeling false; cultivated form can express care more fully than uncontrolled impulse. Music coordinates bodies and emotions, joining people without erasing distinctions. The ideal is a dynamic fit among inner response, outward form, role, resources, and occasion. Mechanical performance misses that fit, but sincerity alone cannot invent a shared grammar each time. Xunzi thus rejects the choice between authentic feeling and social convention: well-made conventions can educate, communicate, and complete human feeling.',
  ],['confucianism','ethics','aesthetics'],[],['Xunzi']),
  section('heaven','Heaven and the division between cosmos and human work',[
    'In “Discourse on Heaven,” tian follows constant patterns without rewarding virtue or punishing vice in a simple providential way. Order and disaster depend importantly on human preparation, agriculture, government, and response. Eclipses, unusual events, rain, and drought need not be decoded as personalized moral messages. Yet translating tian simply as modern “Nature” can erase its inherited religious and political resonance. Xunzi deliberately reworks a charged term. His claim is practical: do not abandon knowable human responsibilities while speculating anxiously about cosmic intention.',
    'The distinction between Heaven and humanity does not make humans independent of the world. Seasons, bodies, resources, and regular processes condition action. Human beings contribute by forming culture, classifications, tools, rites, and institutions that make use of those conditions. Xunzi sometimes calls this forming a triad with Heaven and Earth. The stance is neither worshipful passivity nor fantasy of limitless control. One should understand regularities and arrange affairs accordingly. Ecological readers may value attention to constraint while questioning whether a strongly managerial vocabulary adequately respects nonhuman life. The text opens that conversation without already containing modern environmental science.',
  ],['metaphysics','philosophy-of-science'],[],['Xunzi']),
  section('language','Names, distinctions, and the public conditions of reasoning',[
    '“Rectifying Names” explains naming as a conventional human practice grounded in shared sensory capacities and established to coordinate affairs. Names are not naturally fused to objects, yet neither are they arbitrary at every moment. Communities stabilize usage, distinguish kinds for purposes, and judge deviant innovation against communicative work. Xunzi attacks paradoxes and verbal performances that detach distinctions from practical standards. His conventionalism therefore supports objectivity within disciplined practices: agreement is made, maintained, and answerable to successful differentiation rather than discovered as an eternal vocabulary already written into things.',
    'The chapter also analyzes desires, choice, and claims that action is impossible because desire compels. Xunzi distinguishes having a desire from approving action on it, preserving deliberation without imagining a disembodied will. Correct language helps keep such distinctions available. Political power nevertheless complicates rectification: rulers can impose names, and claims of disorderly language can silence dissent. Xunzi emphasizes sage standards more than open democratic contest. Contemporary use should therefore separate the insight that public reasoning needs stable terms from the dangerous inference that authority alone determines correct speech. Norms of naming require criticism as well as coordination.',
  ],['philosophy-of-language','logic','political-philosophy'],[],['Xunzi']),
  section('politics','Institutions, hierarchy, and the work of government',[
    'Xunzi’s politics joins moral education with administrative and economic order. A ruler needs ritual legitimacy, worthy ministers, clear responsibilities, appropriate rewards and punishments, and policies that support production without exhausting the people. Because spontaneous pursuit can generate conflict, institutions cannot rely on exemplary charisma alone. Yet Xunzi does not reduce rule to techniques of control. The ruler’s own cultivation, public standards, trust, and selection of the worthy determine whether administration serves order or predation. His ideal remains hierarchical, and its graded distributions demand critical rather than celebratory reception.',
    'Later tradition associates Li Si and Han Fei with Xunzi, encouraging a simple story in which his pessimism naturally becomes Legalism. Teacher relationships and intellectual influence are more complicated than that genealogy suggests. Xunzi criticizes rule based only on punishments and techniques, and he treats ritual, education, music, and humane authority as indispensable. Still, his confidence in deliberate institutional shaping can be appropriated in coercive directions. The important comparison asks which mechanisms cultivate judgment and which merely secure compliance. Xunzi offers a robust theory of institutions while leaving unresolved how subjects can contest badly designed standards.',
  ],['political-philosophy','legalism','confucianism'],['han-feizi'],['Xunzi','Han Feizi']),
  section('rivals-reception','A synthetic critic later placed behind Mencius',[
    'Xunzi criticizes named and unnamed rivals for grasping one corner of the Way: Mohist concern with utility, Daoist attention to desire or cosmic patterns, logicians’ paradoxes, and rival Confucian lineages can each become partial fixations. These reports are evidence for Xunzi’s synthetic ambition, not neutral surveys. He inherits Confucian commitments to learning, ritual, music, and exemplary government while transforming their justification through explicit accounts of desire, convention, and institutions. Calling him simply a Confucian “realist” risks making Mencius naïve and Xunzi modern; both respond to political danger with different moral psychologies.',
    'Xunzi was highly influential in early imperial intellectual life, textual learning, ritual theory, and political thought, but later Four Books curricula elevated Mencius as the orthodox account of human nature. Xunzi could then appear as a heterodox counterpoint despite the breadth of his earlier reception. Modern scholarship has renewed interest in his philosophy of language, moral psychology, ritual, natural order, and institutional design. This recovery should not erase hierarchical and gendered assumptions or turn him into a secular liberal. His work matters because it makes culture’s constructive power philosophically explicit and therefore open to evaluation.',
  ],['confucianism','chinese-philosophy'],['mencius','mozi','zhuangzi','han-feizi'],['Xunzi','Mencius','Mozi','Zhuangzi','Han Feizi']),
  section('reading','Reading Xunzi as more than chapter 23',[
    'Begin with “Encouraging Learning,” then read “On the Confucians,” “Discourse on Ritual,” “Discourse on Music,” “Dispelling Blindness,” “Rectifying Names,” and “Discourse on Heaven” before treating chapter 23 as a summary of the whole philosophy. Hutton’s complete translation supplies consistent terminology and notes; compare the received Chinese and chapter divisions through the Chinese Text Project when key words recur. Track how analogies function and distinguish Xunzi’s report of a rival from the rival’s surviving text. No isolated translation of xing, e, wei, xin, li, or tian should bear an entire interpretation.',
    'Then read chapter 23 beside Mencius 2A and 6A. Ask whether each author classifies moral capacities, spontaneous tendencies, and achieved dispositions in the same way. Use the SEP and IEP accounts to compare reconstructions, and Goldin to place arguments within a broader systematic project. Keep ritual, language, Heaven, and politics connected: Xunzi’s moral psychology explains why public forms are needed, while his institutional theory shows what those forms do. The final critical question is double: how can deliberate culture transform destructive patterns, and how can people diagnose when inherited culture itself has become destructive?',
    'A useful final comparison follows the concept of artifice across domains. In learning, acquired practice bends and sharpens capacities; in ritual, designed forms coordinate desire and grief; in naming, conventions stabilize distinctions; in politics, offices and allocations organize collective action. In each case, ask what makes one construction better than another. Xunzi appeals to order, sustainable satisfaction, coherent distinctions, accumulated tradition, and the achievements of sages, but those standards can conflict or protect hierarchy. This question prevents “culture is constructed” from becoming either praise or dismissal. Human making is the source of ethical possibility and also the place where distortion, domination, and critical revision must be examined.',
    'Finally, resist reading every technical term through chapter 23. Li changes significance across ritual pattern and argumentative principle; xin joins cognition and affect; tian retains inherited resonance even when its operations are regular; wei can identify deliberate activity without meaning deceptive artificiality. Build a passage index before settling translations. Then ask where Xunzi grants revision of inherited standards and where he appeals to sage authority. That tension matters for any modern use: a theory that explains how norms are made must also explain how makers and learners can recognize when established norms no longer coordinate flourishing but preserve avoidable domination.',
  ],['confucianism','ethics','political-philosophy'],['mencius'],['Xunzi','Mencius']),
];

